' plan-110 gave `tcp`, `udp` and `tls` resources with the SAME bare names
' (`Socket`, `Listener`). The type checker's bare-name fallback -- written when
' bare names were globally unique, so `File` and `fs::File` could only mean the
' same type -- then equated them, and every binding below type-checked silently.
' A TCP stream bound as a UDP datagram socket is a resource-kind confusion the
' type system exists to prevent; the mismatch surfaced much later as "NIR
' declares unused runtime helper", or not at all.
'
' (plan-110-E Phase 3 removed net's Socket/Listener, so the surviving
' same-bare-name set is tcp/udp/tls. net's absence is pinned separately by
' syntax/net/net_stream_surface_removed_invalid.)
'
' Each of these must be TYPE_BINDING_MISMATCH.
IMPORT tcp
IMPORT udp
IMPORT tls
IMPORT io

FUNC main AS Integer
  RES server = tcp::listen("127.0.0.1", 0)

  ' Same bare name `Socket`, different packages.
  RES asUdp AS udp::Socket = tcp::accept(server, 0)
  RES asTls AS tls::Socket = tcp::accept(server, 0)

  ' Same bare name `Listener`, different packages.
  RES asTlsListener AS tls::Listener = tcp::listen("127.0.0.1", 0)

  ' And the reverse direction, so the check is not one-sided.
  RES back AS tcp::Socket = udp::bind("127.0.0.1", 0)

  io::print("unreachable")
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
